# Hitchhiking Map Project - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This is a Flask-based web application for mapping hitchhiking rides using the Nostr protocol. The project is a fork of the original Hitchmap by Bob de Ruiter and Till Wenke, now maintained by Hitchwiki. It serves as a decentralized platform for sharing hitchhiking experiences and ride data.

## Technology Stack
- **Backend**: Flask with SQLAlchemy ORM
- **Database**: SQLite (production/development)
- **Authentication**: Flask-Security with username-based auth
- **Frontend**: HTML templates with JavaScript, CSS, and Folium maps
- **Data Protocol**: Nostr for decentralized ride data
- **Deployment**: Docker with Apache/NGINX reverse proxy
- **Code Quality**: Ruff for linting and formatting

## Architecture

### Core Structure
- **Flask App Factory**: `hitch/__init__.py` with blueprint registration
- **Database Models**: `hitch/models.py` - User, RideEvent, OsmHitchhikingSpot, etc.
- **Blueprints**:
  - `main` - Map rendering, experience logging, ride submission
  - `user` - User management and authentication  
  - `publish_ride` - Ride publishing to Nostr protocol
- **Extensions**: Flask-Security, Flask-SQLAlchemy, Flask-Mailman
- **Scripts**: Data processing in `hitch/scripts/` (fetch_nostr, show, dashboard, etc.)

### Key Models
- **RideEvent**: Stores Nostr ride events with JSON content and extracted columns
- **OsmHitchhikingSpot**: OpenStreetMap hitchhiking locations
- **HitchwikiArticleLocation**: Hitchwiki article coordinates
- **CoHitchhiker**: Co-hitchhiker acceptance tracking
- **User**: Extended user model with hitchhiking profile data

## Development Guidelines

### Environment Setup
```bash
# Virtual environment
python3 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate

# Dependencies
pip install -r requirements.txt

# Database setup
curl https://hitchmap.com/dump.sqlite > db/points.sqlite
curl https://hitchmap.com/dump.sqlite > db/prod-points.sqlite

# Configuration
cp example.env .env
cp user-password.py.example user-password.py
```

### Flask Commands
- `flask init` - Initialize database and run generate-all
- `flask run` - Start development server
- `flask generate <script_name>` - Run specific script
- `flask generate-all` - Execute all data processing scripts

### Code Quality Standards
- **Linting**: Use `ruff check` (line length: 130)
- **Formatting**: Use `ruff format`
- **Pre-commit**: Configured with ruff hooks
- **Configuration**: `ruff.toml` with pycodestyle, pyflakes, pyupgrade, flake8-bugbear, flake8-simplify, isort

### Database Management
- **SQLite**: Primary database with configurable paths
- **Migrations**: Handled through Flask-SQLAlchemy
- **Data Sources**: 
  - Nostr relays for ride data
  - OpenStreetMap for hitchhiking spots
  - Hitchwiki for article locations

## Coding Standards

### Python Style
- Follow PEP 8 with 130 character line length
- Use type hints where appropriate
- Prefer explicit imports over wildcard imports
- Use descriptive variable and function names
- Add docstrings for public functions and classes

### Flask Patterns
- Use blueprints for route organization
- Implement proper error handling with try/catch blocks
- Use Flask-Security decorators for authentication
- Follow the app factory pattern in `__init__.py`

### Database Patterns
- Use SQLAlchemy ORM for all database operations
- Define models in `models.py` with proper relationships
- Use database sessions properly (commit/rollback)
- Handle database errors gracefully

### Nostr Integration
- Follow the hitchhiking data standard for ride events, https://github.com/Hitchwiki/hitchhiking-data-standard
- Use proper JSON structure for ride content
- Implement proper error handling for relay connections
- Validate data before posting to Nostr

## File Organization

### Directory Structure
```
hitch/
├── __init__.py          # Flask app factory
├── models.py            # Database models
├── settings.py          # Configuration classes
├── extensions.py        # Flask extensions
├── forms.py             # WTForms definitions
├── helpers.py           # Utility functions
├── blueprints/          # Route blueprints
│   ├── main.py         # Main application routes
│   ├── user.py         # User management
│   └── publish_ride.py # Nostr publishing
├── scripts/             # Data processing scripts
├── static/              # Static assets (CSS, JS, images)
└── templates/           # Jinja2 templates
```

### Script Organization
- Data fetching scripts in `hitch/scripts/`
- Use proper error handling and logging
- Implement file locking for cron jobs
- Follow the pattern in existing scripts

## Security Considerations

### Authentication
- Username-based authentication (not email)
- Password hashing with Argon2
- Session management with strict SameSite cookies
- CSRF protection for POST requests

### Data Privacy
- User data is owned by users (can be deleted)
- No commercial use of profile data
- EU-based hosting for data protection
- Aggregate data only for research

### Environment Variables
- Never commit secrets to version control
- Use `.env` files for local development
- Set proper environment variables for production
- Keep OAuth credentials secure

## Deployment

### Docker
- Use provided Dockerfile and docker-compose.yml
- Mount volumes for database and logs
- Set proper environment variables
- Use production WSGI server (Waitress)

### Cron Jobs
- Use `cron.sh` for automated data fetching
- Implement file locking to prevent concurrent runs
- Log all cron job activities
- Handle errors gracefully

### Web Server
- Apache configuration in `apache.conf`
- Enable required modules (proxy, headers)
- Use reverse proxy for Flask app
- Serve static files efficiently

## Data Processing

### Nostr Integration
- Fetch data from multiple relays
- Handle connection failures gracefully
- Validate data structure before processing
- Update database with new ride events

### Map Data Generation
- Generate map views every minute via cron
- Use efficient database queries
- Cache frequently accessed data
- Handle large datasets properly

### Analytics
- Generate dashboard data periodically
- Use proper aggregation queries
- Handle missing data gracefully
- Export data in standard formats

## Testing and Debugging

